\par\par \b \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  To Shrink Its Office Footprint\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Bindley \par \uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Salesforce.com Inc\uc2 . plans for most of its employees to work remotely part or full time after the pandemic and to reduce its real-estate footprint as a result, a top executive said, showing Covid-19's lasting impact on how companies manage their workforces.\par \par \uc2 The business-software provider, which has 54,000 global employees, is among the largest companies to spell out how it plans staff to work after the health crisis recedes. Chief People Officer Brent Hyder said \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 's changes would include revamping office layouts to increase collaboration space instead of having a "sea of desks." As employees are expected to do much of their independent work remotely, the mock-ups of the new offices feature cafe-style seating, open-air conference areas and private nooks, with an emphasis on clean desks and social distancing.\par \par \uc2 "We're not going back to the way things were," Mr. Hyder said in an interview. "I don't believe that we'll keep every space in every city that we're in, including San Francisco."\par \par \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  expects more than 65% of its workforce to come into the office one to three days a week in the future, up from 40% before the pandemic. An unspecified number of additional employees would be fully remote.\par \par \uc2 The company is the largest private employer in San Francisco and occupies the city's tallest building, known as Salesforce Tower, and others with similar names in cities including Indianapolis, New York and Chicago. The company declined to say how much its real-estate footprint might shrink as a result of its changes.\par \par \uc2 Tech companies were among the earliest to tell their employees to work from home when the coronavirus began spreading last year, and some have talked about embracing flexible work permanently. In May, \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 ., which has about 56,600 employees, said it would be moving to a substantially more remote workforce, and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  said it would give all employees the option to stay remote.\par \par \uc2 Others have indicated they remain committed to office-based work.\par \par \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 ., which went on a hiring spree last year, expanded office space in six U.S. cities and said last month that it had leased more space in Boston to accommodate a growing workforce there.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic's persistence has continued to push back timelines for offices to reopen. Some tech workers have opted to move out of major cities such as San Francisco and New York that have long attracted top talent. Both residentialand office rents in San Francisco have fallen during the pandemic. A few companies, including \uc2 Oracle Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Hewlett Packard Enterprise Co\uc2 ., have moved their headquarters from California to Texas.\par \par \uc2 "On the other side of the pandemic I do think we're going to see more innovation in more parts of the country, and I think that's healthy," said \uc2 Bret Taylor\uc2 , Salesforce's chief operating officer, "but it in no way diminishes my excitement for the Bay Area."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hyder said \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  can now do more with less space. He relocated from San Francisco to Southern California last year but said there hasn't been an overall surge in employees asking to move permanently.\par \par \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  hasn't adjusted salaries of employees who relocated to less costly places during the pandemic, but it is continuing to evaluate its compensation strategy. "My suspicion is the market is going to forever be changed in where people are located," Mr. Hyder said. "A great engineer is still going to cost me a good amount of money whether they're located in Louisiana or San Francisco."\par \par \uc2 The company notified about 1,000 employees in August that it was eliminating their jobs, though some have found new ones within the company. The executives said \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  plans to hire more than 12,000 people this year and that the process is now less bound by geography.\par \par \uc2 "Not everyone who is a talented technologist can afford to pack up and move their families out to the West Coast and California," said Mr. Taylor.\par \par \uc2 While the majority of \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  employees would no longer be working in the office full time, the company cited an internal survey in which 80% of employees said they wanted to maintain a connection to a physical space.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210210eh2a00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
