\par\par \b \uc2 As Aid Plan Takes Shape, Biden Backs Off $15 Wage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Duehren \par \uc2 799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Democrats moved forward with a $1.9 trillion relief plan by approving a budget outline for the proposal, which President Biden indicated Friday wasn't likely to include a $15 federal minimum wage favored by many on the left.\par \par \uc2 In both the House and Senate on Friday, Democrats passed a budget resolution, advancing a legislative process that allows the party to pass the relief package with a simple majority. Now, Democrats will turn to ironing out details of the package, including deciding who will be eligible for $1,400 direct payments.\par \par \uc2 A provision to boost the minimum wageas part of the bill isn't likely to pass Senate parliamentary rules limiting policy provisions that can be included, Mr. Biden said in an interview on CBS News that aired Friday evening.\par \par \uc2 "Well, apparently, that's not going to occur because of the rules of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ," Mr. Biden said in the interview about the wage proposal, adding that he would seek to boost the federal pay floor in future legislation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's comments on the minimum wage come as Democrats race to craft details of the White House proposal, aiming to pass a new package before federal unemployment aid expires in mid-March. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  (D., Calif.) told members in a letter Friday that she hopes to finish work by the end of February.\par \par \uc2 The $1.9 trillion plan, the first legislative push Democrats are making since taking control of the government, enhances and extends unemployment assistance; provides funding for vaccine distribution; and sends funds to schools, state and local governments, and child-care providers, among other provisions. Since the pandemic started, Congress has passed roughly $4 trillion in relief to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have argued the proposal is too large and too proximate to the $900 billion package Congress approved in December, which has yet to be fully spent. A group of 10 Republican senators have laid out a $618 billion alternative, which Mr. Biden has called too small.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden met with a group of top House Democrats involved in writing the legislation on Friday, the latest in a series of meetings with lawmakers about the relief bill. At the meeting, House committee leaders presented on various elements of the bill, and \uc2 Rep. Richard Neal\uc2  (D., Mass.), chairman of the Ways and Means Committee, said the group discussed who should receive the $1,400 direct payments in Mr. Biden's plan.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has indicated he is open to more narrowly targeting the checks. Some lawmakers are pushing to lower the income cutoffs for receiving the payments to $50,000 for individuals and $100,000 for married couples, from $75,000 for individuals and $150,000 for couples. Above those levels, the payments would shrink before zeroing out.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers and the president also discussed that Congress would take up an infrastructure package after completing the relief package, with Mr. Biden pointing to House Transportation and Infrastructure Committee Chairman \uc2 Peter DeFazio\uc2  (D., Ore.) and saying "you're next."\par \par \uc2 The talks came as the latest employment report showed the labor market remained weak, with the U.S. economy adding 49,000 jobs in January.\par \par \uc2 The Senate adopted the budget resolution early Friday following an all-night "vote-a-rama" in which Republicans tried to put Democrats on the spot on the pace of school reopenings and raising the minimum wage, among other issues, during a lengthy amendment process. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  cast her first tiebreaking votes in the Senate to advance the resolution.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have criticized the substance of Mr. Biden's package and the Democratic decision to move forward with reconciliation, a tool both parties have used.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Jason Smith (R., Mo.), the top Republican on the House Budget Committee, attacked the proposal's $350 billion in aid to state and local governments.\par \par \uc2 "One piece of this debate that is increasingly obvious and concerning to me is how Democrats are pushing legislation that will rescue or reward their political allies in blue-state capitals across the country," he said. States governed by both political parties have seen their budgets squeezed, and the proposed federal aid would be available to every state.\par \par \uc2 Congress can pass a limited number of reconciliation bills each year, and measures passed under the rules must have budgetary effects that aren't merely incidental to the policy aims. Some Democrats, including Mrs. Pelosi, had indicated they didn't think raising the minimum wage would be possible under reconciliation, while senators including Bernie Sanders of Vermont have pushed for its passage. Other centrist lawmakers had indicated they may not be able to support raising the minimum wage, anyway.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Richard Rubin and Catherine Lucey contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210206eh260002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
