\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: GOP Adds Amendments to Aid Package --- Senators focus on minimum wage, school reopenings in Biden's $1.9 trillion relief plan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Duehren \par \uc2 777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Senate Republicans unleashed a blitz of amendments to President Biden's $1.9 trillion relief plan, seeking to put Democrats on the spot over raising the minimum wage and the pace of school reopenings, among other issues.\par \par \uc2 Under the special procedure Democrats are using to pass the $1.9 trillion plan, Republicans can offer an unlimited number of amendments to the budget resolution the Senate is now considering. Passing the budget resolution will move Democrats forward with reconciliation, a process that allows the Senate to pass legislation with a simple majority. The House approved the budget resolution Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Because the budget is nonbinding and never becomes law, the amendment votes are largely symbolic, a factor that can influence how much support they receive. Republicans offered amendments on cutting federal funding to states that have an active investigation into underreported deaths in nursing homes and blocking aid for schools that don't reopen after teachers have had the opportunity to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 New York Democratic \uc2 Gov. Andrew Cuomo\uc2  is under fire over nursing-home deaths, and several states and cities are grappling with teachers unions, a key Democratic constituency, over when to reopen schools. Both the amendment on reporting nursing-home deaths and on reopening schools failed in the Democratic-controlled chamber.\par \par \uc2 Another amendment in the mix would prevent Congress from raising the federal minimum wage during a pandemic; Mr. Biden's relief plan calls for gradually raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour. His proposal would also bolster federal unemployment assistance, send $1,400 direct checks to many Americans, provide funds for vaccine distribution and offer aid to schools.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.) and a group of bipartisan senators offered an amendment that seeks "to ensure upper-income taxpayers are not eligible" for the $1,400 payments in Mr. Biden's plan. While the amendment doesn't specify an income threshold for the payments and wouldn't carry the force of law, it is another sign of the bipartisan desire to more narrowly target the checks. The Senate approved it 99-1.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has said he is open to offering the payments to a smaller group of Americans.\par \par \uc2 The president is scheduled to meet Friday morning with leading House Democrats and committee heads involved in advancing the relief package.\par \par \uc2 The marathon of amendments, known as a "vote-arama" in the Senate, gives lawmakers a chance to attempt to shape Mr. Biden's plan. For Republicans, it is also an opportunity to score political points.\par \par \uc2 Several amendments to the budget resolution succeeded, though they were symbolic and might not become law. One calls for creating a fund to provide aid for restaurants and dining services, while another moves to prevent tax increases on small businesses during the pandemic. The Senate voted 58-42 to prevent immigrants without permanent legal status from receiving direct payments.\par \par \uc2 Many GOP lawmakers have opposed Mr. Biden's plan because of its cost and proximity to the previous relief package, a $900 billion round passed in December. They have also criticized Democrats' embrace of reconciliation, a tool both parties have previously used.\par \par \uc2 "Let's hope President Biden remembers the governing approach he promised and changes course. In the meantime, if we're to debate this phony, partisan budget, we will create some clarity for the American people," Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell (R., Ky.) said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A group of 10 Republicans floated an alternative aid plan, about a third the size of Mr. Biden's proposal, and met with him at the White House this week. That group sent a letter to the White House on Thursday, questioning the amount of education funding in his proposal and asked for additional data about Mr. Biden's plan.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has said the GOP proposal is too small, and Democrats have largely rallied around the president's plan, arguing that another large relief bill will help shore up the economy and help struggling households and businesses.\par \par \uc2 "I sincerely hope our Republican colleagues approach our work today with the intention of having serious ideas considered, not using the debate over pandemic relief to sharpen ephemeral partisan talking points," Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer (D.,N.Y.) said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 After the amendment process ends, the Senate can vote on the budget resolution. If the Senate approves any changes to the resolution, the House will have to vote on the changed resolution. Once it is passed, committees will begin writing the substance of the relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210205eh250002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
