\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Labor Pick Would Bring Union Advocate to Cabinet\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe \par \uc2 535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Biden's pick for Labor Department secretary, Boston Mayor \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 , would bring a champion of organized labor to the White House cabinet.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walsh, who testified at a Thursday Senate hearing on his confirmation, is expected to pursue the administration's labor goals, including efforts to recover millions of jobs lost last year, expanding who qualifies for overtime pay and pressing Congress to raise the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 He also is expected to pursue the administration's plans to boost labor unions, a promise Mr. Biden made while campaigning for president.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's hearing before the Senate Health, Education, Labor and Pensions Committee brought Mr. Walsh one step closer to a full Senate confirmation vote. Committee Republicans are mostly expected to support Mr. Walsh's nomination, a GOP aide said. Ranking member \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2  (R., N.C.) signaled during the hearing that he would vote in favor of moving the nomination forward.\par \par \uc2 In testimony, Mr. Walsh connected his experiences growing up in an Irish immigrant family, working blue-collar jobs and joining a local union to his advocacy for policies that protect workers. "These are not just policies to me. I've lived them. Millions of American families right now need them," Mr. Walsh said.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Patty Murray\uc2  (D., Wash.), chairwoman of the Senate panel, and other Democrats raised questions about how Mr. Walsh would boost worker protections amid the coronavirus pandemic and work to address challenges minorities and women face in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Republicans pressed Mr. Walsh about his support for increasing the federal minimum wage and raised concerns about how such a move could negatively affect businesses and workers.\par \par \uc2 GOP committee members also signaled they would push back on Democratic-led efforts to pass legislation, known as the PRO Act, that seeks to increase union membership and boost employee protections. Ms. Murray said she would introduce the legislation on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walsh during the hearing said he supports the bill, but that he would be willing to consider opposing perspectives on it.\par \par \uc2 The hearing came against the backdrop of a health crisis that has caused millions of job losses and complicated workplace safety. Mr. Biden has directed the \uc2 Labor Department's Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  to set a temporary standard to require employers to develop coronavirus safety protocols.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walsh on Thursday said work at OSHA, including increasing the number of the agency's inspectors, would be a priority for him.\par \par \uc2 "If we increase standards and don't increase inspectors, then we don't really protect the American worker," Mr. Walsh said.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department has a range of responsibilities, from investigating employers for illegal wage practices and worker-safety violations to conducting statistical analysis and facilitating job training programs. It also has some oversight of unions and oversees registered apprenticeship programs, many of which are run by labor unions.\par \par \uc2 Before his election as Boston's mayor, Mr. Walsh forged strong union ties as president of Laborers Local 223, of which his father was also a member, and as head of the Boston region's Building and Construction Trades Council.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210205eh250001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
