\par\par \b \uc2 Pandemic-Weary Essential Workers Soldier On --- Store clerks, delivery drivers, guards battle exhaustion; 'My guys are tapped out'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Levitz, Valerie Bauerlein and Alejandro Lazo \par \uc2 2,130 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When the state shut down around her in March, 67-year-old Joyce Babineau, a supermarket employee in Dartmouth, Mass., was among the many workers who stayed on the job providing basic needs during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 She felt pride walking into Stop & Shop at 6 a.m. five days a week. She also felt dread knowing she was risking the health of her husband, who had chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.\par \par \uc2 At home after her nine-hour shift, she'd dash to the basement to strip off her work clothes and dump them straight into the washer, and then scrub herself in the shower. Only then would she greet her spouse, Paul Babineau, a 76-year-old \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  veteran so friendly she nicknamed him "the welcome wagon."\par \par \uc2 Now, months later, Ms. Babineau has a new after-work routine. She walks to her fireplace mantel, lights a candle and talks to the gold urn that holds her husband's ashes.\par \par \uc2 "I talk to him and tell him I'm sorry," she says. "Because I brought it home."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Babineau is one of a legion of essential employees who have continued reporting to work during the pandemic to provide for others and keep their communities going. Their own lives have often been anything but normal, with workplace challenges far different from Zoom fatigue and isolation experienced by people working from home.\par \par \uc2 In March, when Americans were just starting to adjust to a locked-down world and no one could have predicted how long the pandemic would last, The Wall Street Journal interviewed essential workers, from a subway operator to a home-health aide. The Journal recently talked to the same group again to see how they have fared in a crisis that has so far led to more than 440,000 U.S. deaths and a transformation of the U.S. economy that is painful for many.\par \par \uc2 The workers expressed a mix of frustration, exhaustion and determination. Most still see the importance of what they do, and some have seized on the demand for services like home deliveries to boost their incomes.\par \par \uc2 Others have stopped working, partly out of fear of getting infected. Others have no choice but to keep going and feel lucky they still have jobs.\par \par \uc2 Despite their exposure, some states are just starting to roll out vaccines for them, after prioritizing the elderly and medical workers.\par \par \uc2 A new study from the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2  found front-line employees, from cooks to machine operators, faced a greater risk of death than other workers during the health crisis.\par \par \uc2 Among coronavirus front-line workers, there is a sense of resilience: They've stuck it out. Yet, there is also some resentment aimed at those who can still work from home, and at a public not fully appreciative of what they do.\par \par \uc2 "As time goes on," Ms. Babineau says, "everybody forgets that you're still on the front line."\par \par \uc2 Carrying the guilt\par \par \uc2 She has worked for two decades at a Stop & Shop supermarket in a coastal village south of Boston, and is in charge of making sure merchandise prices ring up properly at the register.\par \par \uc2 The hypervigilance -- the strict routine to make sure she didn't bring the virus home -- took a toll. Her husband had been practically housebound, skipping church and staying alone while she went to work.\par \par \uc2 But the Babineaus could see an end in sight: 2021 would be the year she would finally retire, she and her husband decided. They would spend more time with their grandchildren and visit the Grand Canyon.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Babineau believes her tragedy took root in the fall. She and a union leader say Covid-19 struck a number of employees, including six department heads, in short order in October and into November.\par \par \uc2 Stop & Shop declined to comment on individual employees for privacy reasons, but says 17 of 156 workers at that store have tested positive for the virus during the course of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The company says it encourages personnel to undergo regular tests and instructs them to stay home if feeling sick. It added that it offers additional paid sick leave for those required to quarantine, and deploys contact tracing if someone does become ill.\par \par \uc2 Before Ms. Babineau realized she was among the infected, her husband started having trouble breathing and landed in the hospital, where he tested positive for Covid-19. Ms. Babineau, who is now 68, then got tested and also was positive.\par \par \uc2 While she quarantined at home with comparatively mild symptoms, Mr. Babineau deteriorated and wound up on a ventilator. By the end of October, he was dead.\par \par \uc2 "I carry the guilt," Ms. Babineau says. "If anything, it should have been me."\par \par \uc2 Now unsure if she can still afford to retire, Ms. Babineau rises at 4:30 a.m. for work each day, putting on her Stop & Shop shirt and now, a heart locket, containing some of her husband's ashes, around her neck. She says she kisses it during her shift.\par \par \uc2 On the subway\par \par \uc2 Sixty miles away, in Boston, Massachusetts Bay Transportation Authority subway operator Dennis Smith says concerns about picking up the virus are now more real to him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Smith, a 30-year veteran of the MBTA, says an employee in the yard where trains are stored unknowingly became infected and wound up passing the virus to two other people.\par \par \uc2 Since the start of the pandemic, nearly 600 MBTA employees, including 90 active cases, have tested positive for the virus, with one employee dying, the MBTA says. The vast majority of affected employees returned to work after recovering, according to the agency, which says its safety measures include protective barriers; supplying masks, gloves and sanitizer; and training supervisors to spot signs of illness.\par \par \uc2 "Bringing it home," says Mr. Smith, who lives with his wife and 13-year-old son. "That's the only thing I'm really concerned about."\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, he's glad he leaves home to go to work, and enjoys helping the janitors and nurses and other Blue Line passengers get to their own essential jobs each day.\par \par \uc2 Far fewer people are riding public transit these days, and he says riders are more on edge than in the past. He sees them getting snippy or even yelling at other passengers who don't socially distance.\par \par \uc2 "I just miss everything being normal," Mr. Smith says. "Just let me go to work. Let me not be breathing into a mask all day, and let me do my job and not be concerned that if someone gets too close and sneezes -- what might be wrong with them?"\par \par \uc2 'Tapped out'\par \par \uc2 At the E.M. Johnson water-treatment plant in Raleigh, N.C., there is still a skeleton crew staffing the facility providing most of the water to the metro area's 600,000 people.\par \par \uc2 Kay Willis supervises the 10-person operations team as they conduct water-quality tests, clean filters and carry out spot checks by walking the property throughout each 12.5-hour shift. Other than a family camping trip for her 50th birthday last fall, Ms. Willis says she has mostly been at work or on call.\par \par \uc2 Months into the pandemic, her people are tired, she says. They routinely pick up overnight or weekend shifts when teammates call in sick or need to quarantine, especially in the weeks after Thanksgiving and Christmas. "It was day by day, who could be here," she says.\par \par \uc2 About half of the plant's 60 employees are still working remotely in order to social distance, and she has had to get comfortable saying "no" when off-site colleagues ask her team at the plant for help.\par \par \uc2 "When other people work remotely, they want to ask my team, 'Can your team pick up this or can your team pick up that?'" she says. "And it's like, they can only do so much. My guys are tapped out."\par \par \uc2 As a supervisor, Ms. Willis has the option to work from home. She says she tried it once and hated it, from the delay in downloading documents in an overtaxed network to the inability to read people's body language.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Willis says she is proud of her crew for pushing through week after week, even though there is scant recognition that maintaining a clean water supply is essential.\par \par \uc2 "They're always unsung heroes," she says. "You know your firefighters, your doctors, your nurses, but you can't run a hospital without water."\par \par \uc2 Delivery crew\par \par \uc2 Vince Haynie, an evangelical Christian pastor, in-home aide and app-based delivery food driver in Chico, Calif., says he's "out and about" every day.\par \par \uc2 He's made more money than ever delivering food through \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , he says. And he has added another income stream by taking patients to doctor visits and dialysis appointments, helping boost his income to as much as $1,200 a week, he says.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Haynie has continued to lead an in-person congregation of as many as 50 people. And as a home health-care aide, Mr. Haynie has continued to care for Teddy Lyon, an elderly disabled man who Mr. Haynie worries might go without food, a clean house or a ride to the pharmacy otherwise. He wears a mask and gloves when in his presence, Mr. Haynie says, although he doesn't when at his church or outside.\par \par \uc2 He says no one at his church has gotten Covid-19. They use a lot of hand sanitizer, he says, and believe that their faith protects them from infection.\par \par \uc2 In San Francisco last spring, delivery driver Nick Lawrence was waking at 6 a.m. to be first in line for orders from app-based shopping companies \uc2 Instacart\uc2  and Shipt. He was on his way to making $100,000 a year. As the pandemic progressed, he says, he became "essentially an alcohol delivery guy."\par \par \uc2 "It wasn't something that made me feel good about myself," says Mr. Lawrence, because his mother and brother both died of alcohol abuse.\par \par \uc2 He dented his car. He smashed his thumb. He lost patience with a customer. And he got nervous as the pandemic continued to rage, as he says he has a respiratory condition placing him at a higher risk of illness from Covid-19. He decided to stop working and applied for unemployment in July. He now only sees his dog and cat.\par \par \uc2 The prison guard\par \par \uc2 Rhode Island correctional officer Richard Ferruccio says that in his 36 years on the prison job, he has been through riots and strikes, but nothing compares to the pandemic. Nationally, detention facilities have struggled to keep the coronavirus at bay.\par \par \uc2 "This has been the most physically and mentally stressful year in corrections that I've had," he says.\par \par \uc2 More than 1,100 inmates and nearly 300 staff members of \uc2 Rhode Island Department of Corrections\uc2  facilities have tested positive for the coronavirus, including a 52-year-old officer who died in December, according to the state agency.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ferruccio says he brought the virus home in November and spread it to his wife and two children, particularly sickening his 21-year-old son, who has since recovered.\par \par \uc2 As the president of the Rhode Island Brotherhood of Correctional Officers union, he hears a lot of concerns. He says one guard, who lives with her elderly mother, had a panic attack while guarding a Covid-positive inmate, a convicted murderer, at the hospital. The inmate was refusing to wear his mask, Mr. Ferruccio says.\par \par \uc2 He says vaccinations now under way are bringing some hope.\par \par \uc2 169 steps\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Duke University\uc2  in Durham, N.C., Joseph Fala continues to climb the 169 steps of the chapel most weekdays to play the carillon. He strikes the bells that ring the 5 o'clock hour and sound a mixture of sacred and secular music, from "I Come With Joy" to "Hedwig's Theme" from the Harry Potter movies.\par \par \uc2 It is an eerie journey. He says it has been an adjustment to play the carillon, the voice of the university, to a place that feels like a shadow of itself, with a limited number of students on campus and only a few people in masks lingering on the quad below.\par \par \uc2 The chapel has broadcast the performance live on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  since late March. Now the chapel also has virtual choirs and concerts, and regular live-streamed services.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fala says he doesn't feel the same heaviness as at the beginning. He chooses from a wider variety of music, rather than leaning on familiar tunes.\par \par \uc2 "Not every day is it, 'We've got to make everyone feel comfortable and cheery,'" he says.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210202eh220001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
