\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Strike Threat Looms Over Chicago Schools\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Barrett \par \uc2 440 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO -- Days before Chicago Public Schools planned to reopen for all elementary school students, a teachers strike could shut down the nation's third-largest school district.\par \par \uc2 The district and union are clashing over how to reopen schools safely, while cases of Covid-19 still ravage swaths of the city. The strike would be the second in two years.\par \par \uc2 The school system started in-person classes three weeks ago for pre-K and special-needs students and plans to launch in-person learning for all elementary schools on Monday. Teachers have been negotiating with the city over how to reopen the schools safely, but no agreement has been reached. \uc2 The Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  told members to stay home Wednesday and Thursday, forcing the schools to cancel those recently restarted classes.\par \par \uc2 The union has said it would strike, if the city retaliates by shutting teachers out of remote-learning software, as it has warned of doing in letters sent to individual teachers. Bargaining talks resumed Thursday and were expected to continue through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Chicago's reopening plans have been among the most contentious nationally, as schools struggle to keep students and teachers safe amid the coronavirus pandemic. The city's overall case rates have been falling, but many areas -- particularly in minority communities -- continue to see high rates of infections.\par \par \uc2 A report by the \uc2 federal Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  Tuesday found that schools can open relatively safely with social distancing, mask requirements and other measures in place. Districts and teacher unions in other cities have also had contentious discussions around reopening.\par \par \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  members voted last weekend to continue with remote learning until it can reach an agreement on the reopening plan.\par \par \uc2 City leaders said they had met with the teachers union more than 60 times over several months to prepare the reopening plan. The city said it has spent more than $100 million on personal protective gear, filters in classrooms, temperature checks, Covid-19 screenings and contact tracing for any cases that do emerge.\par \par \uc2 "I will tell you that I am deeply disappointed that after all this time, all these sessions, all the work to make sure that our \uc2 CPS\uc2  schools and buildings are safe, no agreement has yet been reached," said Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  in a public briefing Tuesday evening.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the mayor said talks were continuing. However, she didn't have a specific update on the progress and declined to speculate on whether the district would lock out teachers from online teaching, if they continue to fail to report to schools.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210129eh1t00028\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
