\par\par \b \uc2 Microsoft Sales Rise 17% Amid Covid-19 Pandemic --- Demand for cloud services, videogaming fuels earnings during work-from-home era\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Tilley \par \uc2 792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 . posted record quarterly sales underpinned by pandemic-fueled demand for videogaming and accelerated adoption of its cloud-computing services during the health crisis.\par \par \uc2 The remote-work era has been a boon for \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . In addition to its videogaming and cloud-computing products, the company has notched strong sales of its Surface laptops as people bought devices to facilitate working from home and distance learning. The use of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Teams workplace-collaboration software, which has been a priority for Chief Executive \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2 , has jumped during the pandemic with its offering of such services as text chat and videoconferencing.\par \par \uc2 "What we have witnessed over the past year is the dawn of a second wave of digital transformation sweeping every company and every industry," Mr. Nadella said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The software giant said fiscal second-quarter net income rose more than 30% to $15.5 billion. Sales advanced 17% to $43.1 billion. Those figures beat Wall Street's expectations of net income of $12.6 billion and sales of $40.2 billion, according to FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  shares were ahead 4% in after-hours trading. The stock has gained more than 40% over the past year.\par \par \uc2 Tech companies broadly have been among the biggest corporate winners during the pandemic and have drawn investor enthusiasm. \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 ., which reports earnings Wednesday, has seen its stock rise about 80% over the past year as people sheltering at home bought Mac computers and iPads. Online retailer \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . has piled up revenue, including in its cloud-computing business that competes with \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's offering.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft finance chief \uc2 Amy Hood\uc2  told analysts Tuesday that further growth is expected in the current quarter. Sales are projected to come in at $40.35 billion to $41.25 billion, the company said. Videogaming should see around 40% growth from a year earlier, with Surface laptop sales also enjoying double-digit increases, Ms. Hood said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nadella's bet on cloud computing has been pivotal to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's multiyear run of year-over-year sales increases. Sales for the company's Azure cloud-services have expanded rapidly. Before the pandemic hit, the pace of growth was slowing as the business gained scale. Remote working arrested that decline. Azure sales increased 50% in the quarter ended Dec. 31, compared with a 48% rise for the prior three-month period.\par \par \uc2 Azure became a bigger source of revenue for \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  than its Windows operating system licenses in the September quarter, said \uc2 Brent Bracelin\uc2 , an analyst at Piper Sandler. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  doesn't break out Azure revenue, but the company is the world's second-largest cloud-computing vendor after \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The role of videogaming in \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's fortunes also has increased under Mr. Nadella, in part fueled by acquisitions. The company last year bought \uc2 ZeniMax Media Inc\uc2 ., the parent company of the popular Doom videogame franchise, for $7.5 billion. Xbox content and services revenue increased 40% in the latest quarter, aided by the November release of two new gaming consoles, Xbox Series X and S, to battle \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 .'s PlayStation 5.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nadella hailed the start for the Xbox consoles, marking "the most devices ever sold in a launch month."\par \par \uc2 Overall gaming revenue was up 51%. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  said revenue in the business segment that includes videogame and laptop activities rose to $15.1 billion, topping even the $14.6 billion its cloud-segment delivered.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , the consoles, a relatively low-margin business, are less important to its bottom line than hooking gamers on subscription services for its games. But the company last week misstepped when it tried to push through a price increase for some of those services. Customers revolted, and the software giant reversed course hours later.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  saw continued strength in its Surface laptop business, with revenue rising 3% as the work-from-home period drags on. The increase, though, didn't match the supercharged 37% sales gain in the prior quarter.\par \par \uc2 "The pandemic has really shown the PC's central role in keeping people connected, productive and secure," said Kyle Vikstrom, a director of investor relations at Microsoft.\par \par \uc2 Revenue from licenses for Windows software rose 1%. Ms. Vikstrom said the result beat \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's internal projections, in matching up against a strong prior-year quarter when customers were upgrading the software after the company began phasing out support for Windows 7.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  said even its advertising business, one of the few segments that lagged early in the pandemic, is showing signs of recovery. Search advertising revenue in the most recent quarter advanced 2% after suffering double-digit declines in the prior two periods.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210127eh1r00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
