\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Biden to Seek Virus Protocols in Workplaces\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon \par \uc2 691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden directed a federal workplace-safety regulator to require employers to develop coronavirus protocols and enforce compliance.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden asked the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  in a planned executive action Thursday to pursue an emergency temporary standard that would set Covid-19 workplace-safety requirements, as part of a series of executive orders this week.\par \par \uc2 "It is critical that the federal government protect the health and safety of America's workers and take swift action to prevent workers from contracting Covid-19 in the workplace," the Biden administration said.\par \par \uc2 Labor and business groups expect OSHA to implement a rule quickly. The process for issuing an emergency standard isn't as involved as the traditional federal rule-making process, in part because it lets government skip the usual public-comment period.\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats and worker advocates support developing a federal rule. Republicans and businesses worry that could become a burden for employers to implement.\par \par \uc2 During the Trump administration, OSHA provided voluntary guidance to employers on how to equip workplaces for the pandemic and didn't pursue virus-specific requirements.\par \par \uc2 David Michaels, an OSHA administrator under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , said such a rule mandating standards would help ensure employers minimize workplace transmission of the virus. "If we want to open the economy and allow it to remain open safely, workers have to be confident they can work without exposure to this deadly virus," said Mr. Michaels, now a George Washington University professor.\par \par \uc2 The proposed rule would likely require employers to create a plan to minimize worker exposure to the coronavirus, policy experts say. Such a plan could entail mandates on masks, physical distancing and ventilation standards. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  currently provides guidelines on these sorts of protections, but employers aren't required to implement them.\par \par \uc2 Employers would likely have to present their plans to OSHA, a subagency of the Labor Department, during a workplace inspection and could be fined for violations.\par \par \uc2 Under the previous administration, the agency conducted investigations and brought enforcement actions "where appropriate" as state and local governments issued restrictions to protect workers, former Labor Secretary \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2  said in a January interview.\par \par \uc2 "OSHA moved very quickly when the coronavirus began spreading to protect workers," Mr. Scalia said. He said he didn't support a federal standard, saying it wouldn't provide enough flexibility to address the virus and evolving knowledge of the disease.\par \par \uc2 OSHA could create the rule without Senate-confirmed leadership in the Labor Department. Mr. Biden has nominated Boston Mayor \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  to become labor secretary, pending Senate approval. Jim Frederick, a former \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  director, was selected as deputy assistant secretary of OSHA.\par \par \uc2 To establish a new rule under the emergency process, OSHA must determine workers are in "grave danger" because of exposure to toxic substances or other hazardous conditions. Once it publishes the planned rule, it has six months to make it final. The rule could be challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 Marc Freedman, vice president of employment policy at the U.S. Chamber of Commerce, said the rule could make employers accountable for how they protect their employees, not just whether they protect them -- which is the current requirement.\par \par \uc2 "That becomes a much more complicated question and opens up a lot more opportunities for enforcement and citations where the employers may very well have been doing what they needed to do," Mr. Freedman said.\par \par \uc2 Some states, such as California, Virginia and Michigan, have implemented their own virus standards. These state plans could offer a framework for a federal effort, policy experts say.\par \par \uc2 California's standard requires employers to craft a written plan on virus prevention. Under the plan, employers must ask workers to report Covid-19 symptoms and possible virus exposure. They also must outline policies for accommodating workers at higher risk of infection and provide information on testing.\par \par \uc2 The NRF and \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2  filed a lawsuit in December against California's standard. It said the state's workforce-safety agency overstepped its authority by requiring paid leave for workers exposed to Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210122eh1m0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
