\par\par \b \uc2 Union, Owners Haggle Over Pay In Market Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate King \par \uc2 727 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Produce handlers and drivers at Hunts Point Produce Market in the Bronx are on strike after contract negotiations with ownership broke down.\par \par \uc2 Hunts Point supplies 25% of New York City's fresh produce, Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 's office said. About 1,400 members of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Local 202 voted to go on strike last weekend, according to the union's president, Danny Kane.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kane said the workers are demanding a $1.60 hourly increase for wages and health-care costs. He said ownership's counteroffer was unacceptable given the health and safety risks many workers have endured during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "Our workers can't be protected by staying home. We have to show up," Mr. Kane said. "You have to put your mask on and wash your hands and hope for the best."\par \par \uc2 Stephen Katzman, owner of S. Katzman Produce and co-president of Hunts Point's cooperative board, said the owners' latest proposal before the strike would have raised total hourly compensation by 92 cents to cover wages and health-care costs in the first year of the contract, 94 cents in the second year and 97 cents the third year. Employers would continue to contribute $15,000 annually toward each employee's health care coverage.\par \par \uc2 The two sides resumed active negotiations Wednesday, Mr. Katzman said. The union declined to comment on whether talks had resumed.\par \par \uc2 This is the first strike by \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  workers at Hunts Point in 35 years, Mr. Kane said. Workers are picketing outside the market, and six were arrested Monday and issued summonses for disorderly conduct and obstructing the roadway, said a New York Police Department spokesman.\par \par \uc2 The market, which operates as a cooperative with 29 owners, handles 210 million packages of produce annually. Workers unload shipments of food pallets, repackage them for distribution and drive trucks to retail, grocery and restaurant customers in the city and across the greater New York region.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Katzman, co-chairman of the ownership's labor negotiating committee, said the market's business has suffered during the pandemic due to restaurant closures and the withdrawal of two major grocery-store chains, \uc2 Fairway Market\uc2  and Best Yet Market. The number of packages coming into Hunts Point was down 10% in 2020 from 2019, he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Katzman said the market and its members have avoided layoffs and spent $3 million on personal protective equipment and other safety measures.\par \par \uc2 "Our expenses went up tremendously during this time," he said. "We kept everybody working. Cash flow became a problem when restaurants closed with no warning. We appreciated everybody, and we did everything that we could to keep everyone safe."\par \par \uc2 A spokesman said the mayor's office hopes the dispute will be resolved quickly, but that there are enough other suppliers in the tri-state area to avoid significant disruptions to the city's food supply in the event of a protracted strike.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Katzman said he thinks a closure of Hunts Point would pose a major disruption to the city's food supply, but that the market remains open during the strike. Owners are using supervisors, family members, temporary workers and some union members who have crossed the picket line to keep the produce moving, he said.\par \par \uc2 New York City Councilman Rafael Salamanca, who is running for Bronx borough president, has called for a federal mediator to assist with labor negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Most union workers at Hunts Point earn $18.57 an hour in base pay, or about $40,000 annually, and receive health and retirement benefits, Mr. Kane said. About 70% of workers live in the Bronx, which has been hard-hit by the pandemic and economic crisis. Many workers got Covid-19 and at least six died from it, he said.\par \par \uc2 John Sorrentino, a 43-year-old tractor-trailer driver at Hunts Point, said his mentor died of coronavirus last spring. Mr. Sorrentino, who earns $21 an hour, said he feels devalued by ownership's resistance.\par \par \uc2 "I know what I deserve and what I should earn," he said. "I feed New York. When you go to your grocer tonight and you get your apple and your bananas and your organic kale, you can think about me and the drivers at Hunts Point Produce Market that bring it in every day and work through a pandemic."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210122eh1m0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
