\par\par \b \uc2 Agencies Rethink Transit Passes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hanan Kolko traveled by train or bus to New York City from his home in Montclair, N.J., for almost 25 years until the coronavirus pandemic forced him to work from home.\par \par \uc2 The 60-year-old labor lawyer can't wait until he receives a Covid-19 vaccine and returns to his commute. But his days of working from an office five days a week are over. So too is his need for a $167 monthly bus pass.\par \par \uc2 "All sorts of things I thought I had to be in the city for, I don't," said Mr. Kolko, who noted that the pandemic has shown how much of his work, such as writing briefs and conducting legal research, can be done remotely.\par \par \uc2 Time-based passes such as weekly and monthly tickets were good value for people like Mr. Kolko who travel about 15 or more days a month. Now those discounted tickets don't make economic sense.\par \par \uc2 Rail and bus agencies are discussing how to lure back riders as the nation emerges from the coronavirus pandemic by devising new incentives and ticket types for workers who may wish to work from home more often.\par \par \uc2 Officials at NJ Transit, New Jersey's statewide rail and bus system, say they hope to launch pilot programs in the coming months that could include rewards programs for regular riders or discounts for bulk purchases of tickets that don't expire. Other agencies have already launched new one- or three-day unlimited passes that better suit people's current commuting needs.\par \par \uc2 Some transit officials say the declining popularity of weekly and monthly tickets predates the pandemic as more workers gravitated toward working a four-day week or at least one day a week from home.\par \par \uc2 Between 2014 and 2019 the share of riders using monthly passes on Metra, Chicago's commuter-rail system, fell 6 percentage points to 59%, according to agency data. During the same period, the share of riders using 10-ride tickets grew 3 percentage points to 25%.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gillis, an agency spokesman, said it is hard to ascribe the decline to a single factor. "We believe at least some of that shift is due to the rise of telecommuting," he said.\par \par \uc2 The share of sales of some weekly and monthly tickets have plummeted during the pandemic. Sales of 30-day unlimited MetroCards, used for travel on New York City's subway and bus systems, fell to 15% of ticket revenues in October, compared with 23% of revenues during the same month in 2019, according to the state-controlled \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MTA\uc2  board members will meet this week to consider possibly eliminating some time-based passes as part of an effort to raise revenue. Some board members are concerned the authority loses millions of dollars because of fraudulent use of such cards.\par \par \uc2 Transit advocates are concerned because they see the passes as essential for low-income riders. A recent survey by the \uc2 MTA\uc2  found riders with a household income of less than $50,000 accounted for 71% of seven-day unlimited and 47% of 30-day unlimited MetroCard sales.\par \par \uc2 "Time-based passes are like the key to the city," said Danny Pearlstein, a spokesman for advocacy group Riders Alliance. "They represent unparalleled access for basic needs to New Yorkers who have no other way of getting around."\par \par \uc2 Commuter-rail passes are usually bought by more affluent riders, many of them white-collar workers. The \uc2 MTA\uc2  runs two of the nation's busiest commuter-rail lines, \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  and Metro-North Railroad, serving suburbs in New York and Connecticut. In recent years, monthly and weekly ticket sales accounted for almost half of fare revenues on both lines, according to agency figures.\par \par \uc2 Rich Andreski, Connecticut's bureau chief for public transportation, said the popularity of time-based passes is likely to decline after the pandemic when, as his department understands, many companies will allow workers to continue to telework at least a few days a week. A recent nationwide survey conducted by \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found more than half of white-collar workers who can work from home would like to do so most or all of the time in the future.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Andreski said working from home even for a few days would be a welcome option for many Connecticut commuters, some of whom travel up to two hours to Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know how an individual goes back to doing that five days a week," he said.\par \par \uc2 He said he would like Metro-North to reconsider how its existing 10-trip tickets are discounted and marketed.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the MTA said the agency is considering new rail and MetroCard ticket types to match the new demands prompted by the pandemic, including multi-trip tickets that could help entice riders back to its systems.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210119eh1j0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
