\par\par \b \uc2 Intel's CEO Change Returns 'Chief Geek' To Technology Giant\b0\par\par\uc2 By Asa Fitch \par \uc2 1,379 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Intel Corp\uc2 .'s incoming Chief Executive \uc2 Pat Gelsinger\uc2  once described himself as the chip giant's "chief geek" -- a role he might need to resume as he seeks to revive the company's prospects and rebuild its technological prowess.\par \par \uc2 The 59-year-old is both an obvious choice to lead America's most iconic semiconductor company -- he spent 30 years there earlier in his career -- and a somewhat unorthodox appointment for a modern chief executive who wrote a book on work-life balance and developed a points system to help himself avoid staying late at the office.\par \par \uc2 Intel\uc2  on Wednesday named the Pennsylvania-farm-boy-turned-engineer to succeed ousted CEO \uc2 Bob Swan\uc2  next month. The appointment marks a turning point for \uc2 Intel\uc2  as it battles market-share losses and weighs its strategic direction, including whether to remain both a designer and maker of chips when most rivals have specialized in one or the other.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger brings the kind of engineering background common at \uc2 Intel\uc2 , one of Silicon Valley's pillars. Mr. Swan, a former financial chief, was only the second CEO in the company's 52-year history not to have a technical background. He remains in his role until Feb. 15.\par \par \uc2 Francois Piednoel, a former Intel engineer who has criticized the company's technical decisions and whose career at the chip maker overlapped with Mr. Gelsinger's, called the new chief a good listener who understood technical problems and made informed decisions. "He is very good at building his confidence by seeking the experts' opinions," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger rejoins \uc2 Intel\uc2  after eight years at the helm of \uc2 VMware Inc\uc2 ., a maker of business software, where he roughly doubled sales. The company's software tools are a key ingredient in the growth of cloud computing, where customers do their digital work on remote servers -- most of which contain \uc2 Intel\uc2  processors.\par \par \uc2 At VMware, Mr. Gelsinger didn't shy away from difficult, company-altering decisions like the ones he could soon face at \uc2 Intel\uc2 . He abandoned the software company's cloud-computing business and opted to profit through partnerships, including with industry leader \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Intel\uc2  he rejoins is much larger than the one he left, but also a bit diminished. While its sales have doubled in the intervening time, \uc2 Intel\uc2  last year lost the title of America's most valuable chip maker to graphics chip maker \uc2 Nvidia Corp\uc2 . \uc2 Intel\uc2  shares fell 17% last year, while \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's more than doubled as demand surged for videogames and other devices where it has an edge.\par \par \uc2 Intel\uc2 's biggest struggles recently have been technological, with repeated delays in rolling out new, more advanced chip technology that have led it to lose out to manufacturing rivals in Asia. \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co\uc2 . and South Korea's \uc2 Samsung Electronics Co\uc2 . have become top producers of the most advanced chips. \uc2 Intel\uc2  has said it is considering outsourcing production of its leading chips, a sharp break from precedent in what could be Mr. Gelsinger's first big decision.\par \par \uc2 Growing up, Mr. Gelsinger loved to tinker with TVs, radios and other electronics and was a top student, according to a cousin, Ernest Gelsinger. An 18-year-old Gelsinger joined \uc2 Intel\uc2  in 1979. He rose up the ranks to become the company's youngest-ever vice president at 30 and its first chief technology officer in 2001 -- effectively making him responsible for upholding Moore's Law, which is named after one of \uc2 Intel\uc2 's founders and posits that the number of transistors on a chip doubles roughly every two years.\par \par \uc2 Friends and associates describe Mr. Gelsinger as an analytical, driven and meticulous leader. Early in his career, during which he received engineering degrees from \uc2 Santa Clara University\uc2  and \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 , his smarts and confidence sometimes rubbed people the wrong way, though he softened those edges while at VMware, said \uc2 David Yoffie\uc2 , a longtime former \uc2 Intel\uc2  board member who has known Mr. Gelsinger since the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 "He's learned how to be much more personable in those kinds of situations where in the past he might have been a little bit brusque," he said.\par \par \uc2 In his three decades at \uc2 Intel\uc2 , Mr. Gelsinger played a key role in designing some of its most successful and lucrative chips, including the 386 and 486 processors that were the computational workhorses inside the personal-computer boom of the 1980s and 1990s. He helped pioneer USB ports and Wi-Fi, both of which are ubiquitous. \uc2 Intel\uc2  was seen as the standard-setter in chip technology. Now rivals such as \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  are more prized, and \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 ., after years of using \uc2 Intel\uc2  chips to power its laptops, dumped the company last year in favor of its own design.\par \par \uc2 Since leaving, Mr. Gelsinger was twice rumored to be an Intel CEO candidate when the job fell vacant, and both times said he didn't want to come back. \uc2 Intel\uc2  Thursday said the new CEO would receive a $1.75 million signing bonus and have a base salary of $1.25 million. He's also eligible for a performance-link bonus that could top $3 million, as well as stock incentives. Mr. Gelsinger said in a memo to employees that he was excited about the technological opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger's return next month comes as chips have seen supercharged demand as the pandemic accelerates spending on digital tools and the chips that power them.\par \par \uc2 "To come back 'home' to \uc2 Intel\uc2  in the role of CEO during what is such a critical time for innovation, as we see the digitization of everything accelerating, will be the greatest honor of my career," he wrote in an email Wednesday to \uc2 Intel\uc2  employees. \uc2 Intel\uc2  and VMware didn't make Mr. Gelsinger available for an interview.\par \par \uc2 The task before him is likely to test Mr. Gelsinger's emphasis on work-life balance. In his 2008 book "The Juggling Act: Bringing Balance to Your Faith, Family and Work," Mr. Gelsinger described how he uses a points system to sort out how much he is home. He awarded himself two points for getting home before 5 p.m. but only one for returning by 6:15 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger also has to contend with competitors that are bulking up. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  plans to buy \uc2 Arm Holdings\uc2 , the British chip-design company, in what would be the industry's biggest deal if it goes through. Longtime rival \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices Inc\uc2 . has said it plans to buy rival chip maker \uc2 Xilinx Inc\uc2 . in an all-stock deal valued at $35 billion.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Intel\uc2  is under pressure to slim down. \uc2 Daniel Loeb\uc2 , activist investor Third Point's chief executive, has criticized \uc2 Intel\uc2 's "failed acquisitions" and urged the company to consider divestitures. Mr. Loeb on Wednesday cheered Mr. Swan's departure to make way for Mr. Gelsinger.\par \par \uc2 The new CEO also faces turmoil in \uc2 Intel\uc2 's engineering ranks. Development problems with its new chip-making technology precipitated the departure of chief engineer Venkata "Murthy" Renduchintala last year and the appointment of new manufacturing and technology executives. The company also lost star chip designer \uc2 Jim Keller\uc2 , who left last June after two years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger has long turned to his faith to help confront business challenges. In his book, he said that a mentor, the fabled former Intel chief \uc2 Andy Grove\uc2 , encouraged him to read The Wall Street Journal. Mr. Gelsinger followed the advice. But when he noticed he sometimes read the Journal before picking up the Bible, he changed course. "I committed to never reading the Journal before finishing my daily devotional time; I'll read God's Bible before reading the businessman's bible," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger delivered a Zoom lecture in June for Workforce Ministries, a group devoted to promoting Christianity in the workplace. He said he has tried to apply lessons from Christian teachings to his leadership at VMware, overseeing employees like a pastor oversees congregants.\par \par \uc2 "Being a Christian CEO of a public company is an extraordinary experience and an opportunity that is one of the highest of my career as I look down on my church of 32,000 employees," he said. His flock at \uc2 Intel\uc2  will be more than three times that size.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210115eh1f0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
