\par\par \b \uc2 The Office Commute Is Gone, So Workers Make One Up --- Missing the routine, people are trying ways to mark the start and end of day\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Levitz \par \uc2 1,052 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For more than a decade, Holly Hein rode the #10 bus 45 minutes to her administrative job at \uc2 Portland State University\uc2  in Oregon. She would plop onto her usual spot at the back of the bus and "space out" with a book. At close of business, she would repeat the ritual in reverse.\par \par \uc2 When the pandemic struck in March, Ms. Hein felt grateful to be able to do her job remotely from home. But in time, something seemed off. "It was just a wrong feeling," she said.\par \par \uc2 She doesn't miss the hassle of commuting, exactly, but yearns for the clear dividing line in her weekdays.\par \par \uc2 "There needs to be a contrast," she said, "to set off 'home Holly' from 'work Holly.'"\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hein embraced a strategy to power through the remaining months of working from home: the pretend commute.\par \par \uc2 Nine months into America's pandemic lockdowns, work-from-home employees who used to slog to the office on the train, bus, ferry or highway are forcing themselves to take short walks, drives or other excursions to re-create commuting and provide a separation between their work and nonwork lives.\par \par \uc2 The buzz-phrase "fake commute" has taken off on social media, with scores of people sharing stories about simulating old routines, including carrying a packed lunch out on strolls. British academic researchers said blurring work-home roles during lockdown could create burnout. "We propose a pretend commute as an intervention," said Anna Cox, a professor at University College London.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hein, now a veteran of the fake commute, has her coffee, puts on "real clothes" and then does one 15-minute loop around the block before getting to her desk by 9 a.m.\par \par \uc2 "The day goes better if I walk around the block before being 'at work,'" she said. "It's almost like a different persona you have in each place."\par \par \uc2 Sacramento sales consultant Eddie Fairchild used to vent about his daily frenzied to-and-fro, which entailed hours in his Toyota Camry navigating the freeway gridlock to make as many as six client meetings a day.\par \par \uc2 He has come to realize the old rat race had its benefits. Now he is working entirely from home, conducting meetings virtually, one right after another with little time between.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, he used the hours stuck in his car between appointments to listen to podcasts and stay in touch with people by phone. The 5:30 p.m. stop-and-go became the perfect time to call "Big Ma," his grandma in Florida. Friday evening highway bottlenecks were for calling his best friend from high school.\par \par \uc2 "I never thought there would come a time when I would miss my downtime from sitting in traffic," he said. "I did not see that coming."\par \par \uc2 After months of working at home, to re-create some semblance of the old routine, he created a faux commute.\par \par \uc2 Two mornings a week, he drives from his home in the suburbs to downtown for coffee -- passing plenty of coffee shops closer by.\par \par \uc2 Fewer workers on the road means less traffic, but there are still some commuters, and he aims to leave around 7 a.m. so he'll "hit some sort of traffic."\par \par \uc2 On a recent day, he was excited when a traffic jam forced him to get off the freeway and readjust his route on the fly.\par \par \uc2 "I thrived in that," he said. "It was the weirdest thing ever."\par \par \uc2 He has scheduled an "evening commute" for a third day, driving downtown after work to get takeout, with his "whole goal to be in the 5:30 rush."\par \par \uc2 Kyle Ashley used to consider cycling to work at a Toronto, Canada, ad agency every day as part of his identity. He would share harrowing tales of cars cutting him off and him screaming at drivers.\par \par \uc2 Then his workplace shrank to his apartment.\par \par \uc2 "One morning I woke up and said something has got to give," he said. He rode his bike from his bedroom to his living room, which was about 7 feet away.\par \par \uc2 Now, he showers, dresses and leaves his house by 8 a.m. for a regular make-believe commute.\par \par \uc2 "I go for a walk around the block to psychologically trick myself that I've left the house for work," he said. It keeps him from "living in a groundhog situation."\par \par \uc2 The abrupt shift to remote work brought challenges, British researchers from schools including \uc2 University College London\uc2 , \uc2 University of Birmingham\uc2  and Northumbria University said in a recent paper, popularizing the pretend commute there. Many workers have simply used the time they would have been commuting to work more.\par \par \uc2 The authors called for people to "reclaim" that former commuting time.\par \par \uc2 Still it can be tricky to replicate commuting pastimes at home. Frank J. Oteri, an editor at a digital music magazine, found that being an anonymous commuter crammed onto a New York City subway every day inspired him to devour book after book.\par \par \uc2 "No one is calling. No one is emailing -- or maybe they are, but I'm not getting a signal," he said of his subway time.\par \par \uc2 Now working from home, he designated 7:30 a.m. to 8:30 a.m. as his "dedicated reading time." But at home, the constant possibility of being able to do other things makes it hard to lose himself in a book.\par \par \uc2 "There are books I've started in the pandemic that I have not been able to complete," he said. "That used to not happen. I would stay until the bitter end."\par \par \uc2 Beth Kanter, a nonprofit consultant in San Jose, Calif., said she hasn't spent so much time at home in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 "Initially, I was definitely overworking," she said. "It was, 'Do I live at work or do I work at home?'"\par \par \uc2 Her solution: Each morning, she ventures outside -- "I visit my plants" -- and then walks the neighborhood admiring different trees and architecture.\par \par \uc2 At the day's end, her "shutting-down ritual" includes putting her laptop in a drawer. Then she and her husband head out for a walk.\par \par \uc2 "It's a space to shut off your 'work mind,'" she said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210112eh1c0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
