\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Careers & Leadership: Smoothing The Transition --- Working from home has helped transgender employees adapt to their new identities\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francesca Fontana \par \uc2 912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For those who decide to come out as transgender, the workplace can be a big hurdle -- using the bathrooms, keeping up a new appearance and dealing with reactions from co-workers.\par \par \uc2 But with many offices going remote, more transgender employees are concluding they can come out while letting their work -- not their gender identity -- speak for them.\par \par \uc2 River Bailey, 41-year-old software developer, says she doesn't know if she would have come out as trans at work if not for her ability to work from her home in central Texas. "It gave me the freedom to just be able to exist," she says.\par \par \uc2 She made the move in late 2019 via a Slack message to colleagues at her all-remote software-consulting agency. She considers herself lucky to work in a sector that can offer remote positions, because most of the trans people she knows have jobs in stores, warehouses, and restaurants.\par \par \uc2 "Before I was even considering [coming out at work] I checked the company's policies, the code of conduct and various other policies that the company has to make sure that identity and gender expression and things like that were covered," she says, adding that the company's human-resources team tweaked the guidelines based on her feedback. While she was nervous when sending her message, she says, it helped that everyone was physically remote.\par \par \uc2 "I could make a statement that was vulnerable and uncomfortable in the safety of my office here at home, and then I could step away from the computer for a little bit and calm down," she says, adding that she was met with nothing but support from her co-workers.\par \par \uc2 Fen Slattery, who works for a website-consulting firm in Chicago, was stressed about transitioning at work before the pandemic. Mx. Slattery, who uses the gender-neutral identifier, is a nonbinary trans person, which generally describes people whose gender identity falls outside of the descriptor of man or a woman. Because of this, the 31-year-old uses the pronouns they, them and their.\par \par \uc2 Before starting masculinizing testosterone therapy, which lowered the pitch of their voice, Mx. Slattery found it stressful to talk to co-workers. "If I had to describe it, imagine going to work when having people hearing you talk and seeing you is immensely painful," Mx. Slattery says.\par \par \uc2 Then, Mx. Slattery had some flexibility to work remotely when beginning to transition in 2019, increasingly working from home while juggling trips to the courthouse for name changes and doctor visits. Mx. Slattery was able to keep in touch with colleagues via Slack and avoid video calls, which they said made it easier for everyone to focus on work.\par \par \uc2 About 1.4 million U.S. adults, or roughly 0.6% of the population, identify as transgender, according to the Williams Institute, a think tank focused on LGBT legal and policy issues based at the UCLA School of Law.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, transgender adults in the U.S. were more likely to be unemployed or have poverty-level income than the general U.S. population, according to the institute's most recent U.S. Transgender Population Health Survey. The annual unemployment rate for transgender adults averaged 12% from 2016 to 2018, the survey found, more than double the average unemployment rate in the general population reported by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  from the same period.\par \par \uc2 Still, the work-from-home shift has shown how successful remote work can be, says Carol Cochran, a vice president at the remote job-listings website FlexJobs. She says offering remote jobs can help level the playing field for underrepresented workers, such as those who are LGBT, nonwhites and disabled employees.\par \par \uc2 It "gives them at the very least a running chance to let their talent speak first," Ms. Cochran says. Remote workplaces can also help eliminate unconscious bias in hiring, for example, when interviews are conducted over the phone instead of via video interviews.\par \par \uc2 In Grand Rapids, Mich., Kiri Stewart says being stuck at home has helped her learn more about her gender identity. Having come out as a trans woman years ago, Ms. Stewart, 39, now identifies as a nonbinary trans woman, which generally describes people who identify with a feminine gender identity more than a masculine one.\par \par \uc2 In March, she started working from home in her job as a graphic designer for a United Methodist church. When she was hired several years ago, she had transitioned to identifying as a woman in her personal life but not at work. She came out to co-workers about five months into the job.\par \par \uc2 Her transition went well, but early on, colleagues asked if her androgynous appearance would change much and what kind of clothes she would wear. She didn't know how to answer them. Friends shared similar experiences, like being asked to keep their gender expression "from going too over-the-top" while at work.\par \par \uc2 "If you try to be very feminine as a trans woman, you're often seen as a caricature or stereotype, but if you don't try then you're just seen as a man or as a trans person who isn't trying hard enough," she says. "Working from home lets you remove yourself from the constant struggle of trying to meet those standards and focus on who you are."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210111eh1b00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
